in hamburg at the bernhard knocked  institute for tropical medicine  scientists are studying mosquitoes that  transmit the dengue virus  some of the insects come from brazil  they're kept in incubators at a constant  temperature of 25 degrees celsius  the researchers have developed a method  of separating mosquitoes that carry the  pathogen from those that do not  they've been collaborating on the  project with the federal university of  rio de janeiro  our goal is to produce a map ahead of  the 2016 olympics that shows the  dangerous areas  so we can say  here the mosquitoes are more likely to  carry dengue  then we can combat them in a more  targeted manner  in the high security lab the scientists  also infect mosquitoes with the dengue  virus  they want to find out which of the  insects are particularly susceptible to  the virus  and how fast the virus  spreads to catch the mosquitoes in  brazil more effectively the researchers  use a special trap  it contains substances that attract the  species that transmit dengue  which of the trapped insects are  actually infected with the virus  that's shown by an automatic test run by  this machine that the scientists have  developed  the analysis takes just two hours the  test shows that this sample is infected  the method can also be used to test for  dengue virus in humans
 

